(*detachment*, *retardation*). The Modifier cases (\"ovarian cancer\" in \"ovarian cancer cases/population\") are attributive uses inside larger phrases. None of the controls has a coordinator linking two modifiers over one shared head.\n- **Isolate Trigger:** The span \"intraretinal and subretinal lipid accumulation\" is a coordinated structure. Two location modifiers, *intraretinal* and *subretinal*, share the head *lipid accumulation*, which the sentence presents as the elliptical form \"intraretinal lipid accumulation and subretinal lipid accumulation.\" The span therefore denotes two distinct located findings. The model likely keyed on the pathological semantics of the context (\"massive... exudative retinal detachment\") and of *accumulation*, and mapped the whole phrase onto the DiseaseClass category.\n- **Hypothesis Check:** Coordination alone does not explain the error. In the control \"breast or ovarian cancer,\" the coordinated disease names are not annotated as CompositeMention. The head type matters too. *Cancer* is a disease-category head, while *lipid accumulation* is a process/finding head with no disease-name status. The combined trigger is a coordinated modifier structure over a non-disease, finding-type head. With only one discrepancy in this cluster, this rule is a hypothesis to validate against more examples.\n\n**Step 2: Formulate Pattern**\n\nThe error arises when a coordinated noun phrase is built from anatomical modifiers over a shared finding/process head, and the model assigns DiseaseClass because the phrase sounds pathological. The gold standard treats this as a composite of two referents rather than a single disease class.\n\n**Output:**\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Coordinated Location Modifiers over a Shared Finding Head (Pathology-Semantic Misfire)\n- **Confusion Trigger:** A coordinator (\"and\") links two anatomical modifiers (\"intraretinal\", \"subretinal\") that share an elided finding/process head (\"lipid accumulation\"). The head is